#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "159b4d58-b34d-52cd-aad2-2c058f772ad2" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "b7051104-6e23-5ab2-8779-5d54a3c3f5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-270" :question/problem "Consider a spherical balloon with radius $r$. As the balloon loses air, its volume $V$ decreases. What is the best interpretation of $V'(r)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/296/Source/Images/fe67280942729a8d2c75f11df00d3dde.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function $V(r)$ gives the volume of a sphere with radius $r$. So\n\n$$\nV'(r) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}V}{\\textrm{d}r} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Change in}V}{\\textrm{Change in}r}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $V'(r)$ is the rate at which the volume changes as the radius changes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2451a654-2255-5867-871d-54340dfeff81" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function in a Given Context"}] :question/id #uuid "8378ead2-9247-55a0-acc8-2e6d0874ea09" :question/math-academy-id "e-271" :question/problem "The function $B(s)$ models a school's budget, in dollars, given the average number of students $s$ in each grade. What is the best interpretation of $B'(30)=2\\,000$?" :question/worked-solution "The function $B'(s)$ represents the rate at which the budget is changing at the moment when the school has an average of exactly $s$ students in each grade.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB'(30) = 2\\,000\n$$\n\nmeans:\n\n> *When the school has an average of $30$ students in each grade, its budget is increasing at a rate of $2\\,000$ dollars per student.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063951 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7df31c9-b27a-5ce2-93c8-178ba96da000" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "750e7350-1434-56ef-80d8-7cf531108f96" :question/math-academy-id "e-273" :question/problem "On a particular day, the rate $E$ at which tourists enter a small country can be modeled as $E(t) = 50\\cdot 3^{0.2t}$ tourists per hour, where $t$ is the time, in hours, after midnight. The rate at which tourists leave the country can be modeled as $L(t)=100\\cdot 2^{0.2t}$ tourists per hour, where $t$ is also the time in hours after midnight.\n\nWhat is the rate of change in the number of tourists in the country at $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$?" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of the number of tourists in the country at time $t$ is equal to the entry rate minus the exit rate at time $t$, that is,\n$E(t)-L(t)$.\n\nSince $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$ is ten hours after midnight, we have $t=10$. Substituting $t=10$, the rate of change in the number of tourists in the country at $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE(10) - L(10) &= 50 \\cdot 3^{0.2(10)} - 100 \\cdot 2^{0.2(10)} \\\\\n&= 450 - 400 \\\\\n&= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the number of tourists in the country at $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$ is $50$ tourists per hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064027 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "5831f44c-02c6-5a5b-a9e7-58c7ac36cb6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6112" :question/problem "A rocket is at a distance of $r(t)$ meters from the ground $t$ seconds after it is launched. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$?" :question/worked-solution "We have that the height $r(t)$ is measured in meters and the time is measured in seconds.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}r(t): & meters \\\\ t: & seconds\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nmeasures a change in $r(t)$ over a change in $t$. So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dr}{dt}: \\mid \\frac{meters}{seconds}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nis measured in \"meters per second\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064028 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "0f4baedc-a28a-5b45-97d7-d7e1196b21c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6113" :question/problem "Howard, the manager of a sci-fi convention, uses the following three functions to model the daily number of visitors to the convention, $t$ minutes after the convention has opened.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of people inside the convention\n- $E(t)$ - the rate at which people enter the convention\n- $L(t)$ - the rate at which people leave the convention\n\nWhat is the relationship between the three functions above?" :question/worked-solution "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of people inside the convention $t$ minutes after opening. Then,\n\n$$\nN'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}N(t)}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Change in}N}{\\textrm{Change in}t}\n$$\n\nThus, $N'(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of people inside the convention changes $t$ minutes after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of people inside the convention at time $t$ by finding the difference between the entry and exit rates at time $t$. That is,\n\n$$\nN'(t) = E(t) - L(t)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "248ff954-3b50-5fd5-a294-d5f37186636b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "squared kilograms per cupcake" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242690} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c54b0e-1734-5619-8a17-4f81f555d0ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "grams per cupcake" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242691} {:answer/id #uuid "963f65ac-5210-5325-a283-dce46558337d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "kilograms per cupcake" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242692} {:answer/id #uuid "639d62e7-ab3f-5cf3-abe0-32d898e8be25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "cupcakes per kilogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242693} {:answer/id #uuid "58713d47-116e-5375-a164-b935853f9031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "cupcakes per gram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4c54b0e-1734-5619-8a17-4f81f555d0ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "grams per cupcake" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242691} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9da1b1d-2037-5b3f-86ca-ef7a872c24d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e565ca9-f380-5310-8209-2c03b355bc43" :question/math-academy-id "q-110037" :question/problem "Mary bakes $c$ cupcakes using $f(c)$ grams of flour. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have that the amount of flour $f(c)$ is measured in grams and the number of cupcakes is measured in cupcakes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c): & \\text{grams} \\\\ c: & \\text{cupcakes}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}c}$ measures a change in $f(c)$ over a change in $c.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}c}: & \\frac{\\text{grams}}{\\text{cupcake}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}c}$ is measured in \"grams per cupcake\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35384093-49f8-5095-975c-6ce2ddcfde41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dollars per liter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242697} {:answer/id #uuid "812be774-ef9e-5fe2-97e3-7f604a3e2bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "cubic dollars per squared liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242698} {:answer/id #uuid "bf32f833-c1b4-5006-a3d5-13e84d6fd7c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "milliliters per squared dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242699} {:answer/id #uuid "c34554b7-41e9-581b-9c78-0302f52d9ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "liters per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242700} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6f9cfb-609f-5074-b1ea-65686cb3a251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dollars per milliliter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35384093-49f8-5095-975c-6ce2ddcfde41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dollars per liter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242697} :answer-field/id #uuid "883daeec-8f2f-55d5-802d-de091780489d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d881f6ea-8d1b-5e89-b8cd-c39ed4f27281" :question/math-academy-id "q-110038" :question/problem "A dairy farm sells milk on a particular day for $p(v)$ dollars, where $v$ is the number of liters of milk in the dairy farm at the start of the day. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}v}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have that the price $p(v)$ is measured in dollars and the volume of milk is measured in liters. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(v): & \\text{dollars} \\\\ v: & \\text{liters}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}v}$ measures a change in $p(v)$ over a change in $v.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}v}: & \\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{liters}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}v}$ is measured in \"dollars per liter\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78f7cd9b-6556-5dfd-87cd-b363bf19c86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "minutes per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242704} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb9464f-e925-549e-8470-59faec1fc4ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242705} {:answer/id #uuid "2b59a1b3-5ded-5da9-840f-8d94bd07652a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "hours per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242706} {:answer/id #uuid "688fab81-afa4-53d7-bdbf-9cb44efd4d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "squared hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242707} {:answer/id #uuid "c965b436-ff26-50e9-81d6-3260bf3ff6c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b59a1b3-5ded-5da9-840f-8d94bd07652a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "hours per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242706} :answer-field/id #uuid "adcdf2dc-f91f-5a27-b18e-901cd4c60f55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9d0d9e7-ccd0-50be-856d-8412d5015d6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-110042" :question/problem "A battery lasts $L(t)$ hours when it is charged for $t$ minutes. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have that the battery duration $L(t)$ is measured in hours and the charging time is measured in minutes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t): & \\text{hours} \\\\ t: & \\text{minutes}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}$ measures a change in $L(t)$ over a change in $t.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{minute}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}$ is measured in \"hours per minute\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2451a654-2255-5867-871d-54340dfeff81" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function in a Given Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "864a91d3-feea-59f8-8d7f-040139534713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ minutes after the observations began, the area of the sheet decreases at a rate of $7$ squared centimeters per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242711} {:answer/id #uuid "8719c86f-60cc-5e58-a3c6-2112e0685dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ minutes after the observations began, the area of the sheet is $7$ squared centimeters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242712} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc99222-4de4-530e-8be3-c2345a8f3e04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ minutes after the observations began, the area of the sheet is $5$ squared centimeters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242713} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2495b8-9e07-52fc-9742-82fb95d3b791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ minutes after the observation began, the area of the sheet increases at a rate of $5$ squared centimeters per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242714} {:answer/id #uuid "9f73903f-8768-51e7-8b00-766cb2950911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "During the first $5$ minutes of the observations, the area has constantly decreased at a rate of $7$ squared centimeters per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "864a91d3-feea-59f8-8d7f-040139534713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ minutes after the observations began, the area of the sheet decreases at a rate of $7$ squared centimeters per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242711} :answer-field/id #uuid "7df7935b-2f86-5564-bd5f-d1d05a4e6f95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d99c466a-50e3-5f69-970e-e212baf18b71" :question/math-academy-id "q-112091" :question/problem "The function $S(t)$ gives the area, in squared centimeters, of a thin sheet of ice as it melts $t$ minutes after observations start. What is the best interpretation of ${S}^{′}(5)=-7?$" :question/worked-solution "The function ${S}^{′}(t)$ represents the rate at which the area of the sheet is decreasing $t$ minutes after the beginning of the observation.\n\nTherefore, since it is negative, ${S}^{′}(5)=-7$ means:\n\n$\\,$ *$5$ minutes after the observations began, the area of the sheet decreases at a rate of $7$ squared centimeters per minute.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7df31c9-b27a-5ce2-93c8-178ba96da000" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6815bf6-c5f8-51a8-92c1-d4a939c88360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.736$ muons per microsecond" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242718} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6fc2a1-6cb1-5bad-972f-1a7cef7b6c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.346$ muons per microsecond" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242719} {:answer/id #uuid "3c645474-0ee1-5e40-8a2c-045be0f0c6de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.311$ muons per microsecond" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242720} {:answer/id #uuid "ca761714-709e-5c96-82f7-01c36fc1aab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.566$ muons per microsecond" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242721} {:answer/id #uuid "87df36c0-7ca3-5514-9047-13ceda62b804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.899$ muons per microsecond" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6815bf6-c5f8-51a8-92c1-d4a939c88360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.736$ muons per microsecond" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242718} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f3adfba-33de-50ae-a048-a75334486e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7487c467-8c5c-52ae-b53d-e72eead10381" :question/math-academy-id "q-112381" :question/problem "The decay rate $M$ of muons in a beam traveling at high speed can be modeled by the function $M(t)=2\\cdot {e}^{-2t},$ where $t$ is the time, in microseconds, since observations started. What will be the decay rate of the muons after $0.5$ microseconds? Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The function $M(t)$ models the decay rate for the particles $t$ microseconds since observations started.\n\nThus, the decay rate of the muons after $0.5$ microseconds is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(0.5) & =2\\cdot {e}^{-2(0.5)} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{-1} \\\\  & ≈0.736.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the decay rate of the particles is $0.736$ muons per microsecond."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7df31c9-b27a-5ce2-93c8-178ba96da000" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80d0727c-d697-51f6-a221-b671f6b60ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ percent per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242725} {:answer/id #uuid "8f227591-2d70-5edc-b3a8-8d4e41fd1ba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ percent per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242726} {:answer/id #uuid "78ed3e20-2e42-5b65-9e73-dd33c8efb962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ percent per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242727} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4d5967-cac6-5cb1-b155-8ef99342a97d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ percent per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242728} {:answer/id #uuid "46578e61-27e6-5678-b460-e7ff8dab9fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$40$ percent per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78ed3e20-2e42-5b65-9e73-dd33c8efb962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ percent per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242727} :answer-field/id #uuid "224f580d-b61d-5dc1-9b8b-cea24b724140" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a8ec250-15a4-5b84-beef-886aee8058ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-112383" :question/problem "After charging the battery of a PC, the percentage of charge decreases at a rate of $c(t)=100\\cdot {5}^{-0.2t}$ percent per hour, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the battery had completed its charge. What will be the rate of decrease after $5$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to determine the rate of decrease in the amount of charge after $5$ hours, we have $t=5.$\n\nThus, the rate of decrease of the amount of charge after $5$ hours will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c(5) & =100\\cdot {5}^{-0.2(5)} \\\\  & =100\\cdot {5}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{5} \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of decrease will be $20$ percent per hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7df31c9-b27a-5ce2-93c8-178ba96da000" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b768dec3-61b6-50d3-91c6-fba939ea8138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$120$ cars per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242732} {:answer/id #uuid "a16de123-27ad-566c-a3a7-01bdace48009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$140$ cars per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242733} {:answer/id #uuid "ee17553b-b1d7-54b4-b1dc-91f74258533f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$100$ cars per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242734} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3b2933-f9b2-5c2a-b258-cc5c785025b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$125$ cars per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242735} {:answer/id #uuid "c0698e01-e0db-5fec-9808-bbb3e13dd709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$162$ cars per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b768dec3-61b6-50d3-91c6-fba939ea8138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$120$ cars per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242732} :answer-field/id #uuid "c06d622e-3b87-5d36-b691-d6ace7cf4eec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac818ad1-0395-53d8-9d76-66c7ed2e0c01" :question/math-academy-id "q-112507" :question/problem "The rate $O$ of the orders a car factory receives can be modeled as $O(t)=100t{e}^{-0.1t}$ cars per day. The rate at which cars are completed can be modeled as $C(t)=50+10sin⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7}t)$ cars per day. In both cases, $t$ is the time, in days, since the launch of the new model. \n\nWhat is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction $28$ days after the model's launch? Assume that an ordered car goes into construction as soon as the factory receives its order. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of the number of cars under construction at time $t$ is equal to the order rate minus the completion rate, that is,\n\n$$\nO(t)-C(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=28,$ the rate of change in the number of cars under construction after $28$ days is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(t)-C(t) & =100(28){e}^{-0.1(28)}-(50+10sin⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7}(28))) \\\\  & =100(28){e}^{-2.8}-50-10sin⁡(8\\pi ) \\\\  & =100(28){e}^{-2.8}-50 \\\\  & ≈120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the number of cars in construction after $28$ days is $120$ cars per day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e749ba7-2a95-53a1-beb1-c7ab58544a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{P}^{′}(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242739} {:answer/id #uuid "f99d5b65-75fc-5ec7-b3d7-d6f13be924c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)={r}^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242740} {:answer/id #uuid "0280d6a3-b467-54c9-8024-31fec04a6152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)={r}^{2}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242741} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a7e8a3-2047-5cab-9a10-bf91c19b648f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{P}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{r(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242742} {:answer/id #uuid "51b71153-c97e-54d7-bf25-9e57d03cf4ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{P}^{′}(t)=1-r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e749ba7-2a95-53a1-beb1-c7ab58544a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{P}^{′}(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242739} :answer-field/id #uuid "82476130-1441-5ecb-9866-4e1520954c05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64fb28a9-2489-5d93-bbf0-bc7405527cc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-112518" :question/problem "Liam, a farmer, is monitoring the construction of a fence by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in hours, since he started his observations:\n\n- $P(t)$ - the number of panels in the fence.\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the number of panels in the fence increases.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $P(t)$ and $r(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $P(t)$ gives the number of panels in the fence after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}P(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }P}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${P}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of panels increases at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $P(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${P}^{′}(t)=r(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd5d23b-7fd4-55e2-ab62-ded476aa5010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)-L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242746} {:answer/id #uuid "296f76fd-6319-56a4-aa83-ebed27df183c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)+L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242747} {:answer/id #uuid "b072e35d-02a9-578c-8c7c-e555852d3444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)\\cdot L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242748} {:answer/id #uuid "bd387453-1f0c-5d5b-9d55-26a3241283eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)=E(t)+L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242749} {:answer/id #uuid "f4659473-5e7a-5d28-8400-3d980239e437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)=E(t)-L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fd5d23b-7fd4-55e2-ab62-ded476aa5010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)-L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242746} :answer-field/id #uuid "69c35e22-e8c0-507c-8873-0d6151eb8169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7e1b45c-7d22-59a8-850d-03ed8a91f836" :question/math-academy-id "q-112519" :question/problem "To measure the number of children visiting a playground, Noah uses the following three functions that model the hourly number of visitors to the playground $t$ hours after the playground is opened to the public.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of children inside the playground\n\n- $E(t)$ - the rate at which children enter the playground\n\n- $L(t)$ - the rate at which children leave the playground\n\nWhat is the relationship between the functions above?" :question/worked-solution "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of children inside the park $t$ minutes after opening. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }N}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of children inside the playground changes $t$ hours after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of children inside the playground at time $t$ by finding the difference between the entry and exit rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)-L(t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "413b05fa-e342-53be-a5f8-83ea32577a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=O(t)+C(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242753} {:answer/id #uuid "c089b227-d3c4-5ac7-9391-50815157d2b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)=-O(t)-C(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242754} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8d77fd-2943-5937-912b-45ff4b1cdf58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)=O(t)+C(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242755} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6cc255-9124-546e-b72f-4b07c8de0ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)=O(t)-C(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242756} {:answer/id #uuid "43dc12eb-2b95-5cba-b1ee-29e83088f68d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=O(t)-C(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43dc12eb-2b95-5cba-b1ee-29e83088f68d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=O(t)-C(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242757} :answer-field/id #uuid "acd7df3c-2bce-5ea3-88dc-2ed2ccb3ceef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73f18b8c-1751-5485-a092-111f7e091ec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-112522" :question/problem "Oliver, the owner of a platform for chess players, uses the following three functions to model the daily number of users of the platform $t$ days after it was launched.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of users with an account\n\n- $O(t)$ - the rate at which new users open an account\n\n- $C(t)$ - the rate at which users cancel their account\n\nWhat is the relationship between the three functions above?" :question/worked-solution "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of users $t$ days after the platform is set up. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }N}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of users with an account changes $t$ days after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of users at time $t$ by finding the difference between the open and cancel rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=O(t)-C(t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2451a654-2255-5867-871d-54340dfeff81" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function in a Given Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4c0a9b9-4721-5a6d-9be2-eb48f55b7237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ hours after the launch, there are exactly $650$ users." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062976} {:answer/id #uuid "e792de43-0f2d-544d-8027-29a196ced420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The number of users increased by $650$ during the first $4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062977} {:answer/id #uuid "06f6ff7b-8e7a-5020-aa4d-97dd0260d642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The number of users increased at a constant rate of $162.5$ users per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062978} {:answer/id #uuid "16c0ac5d-f69e-56ff-8b67-0f900542d44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ hours after the launch, the number of users decreased at a rate of $650$ users per hour\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"determining-a-rate-of-change\"></a>" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062979} {:answer/id #uuid "77180079-d085-5eee-a7f1-76f92c83605d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ hours after the launch, the number of users increased at a rate of $650$ users per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062980} {:answer/id #uuid "81eea91d-8381-50e4-b2c5-9f8ccded11c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ hours after the launch, the number of users decreased at a rate of $650$ users per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77180079-d085-5eee-a7f1-76f92c83605d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ hours after the launch, the number of users increased at a rate of $650$ users per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062980} :answer-field/id #uuid "10140cc1-8b23-58e0-93a5-8a495cf0fc48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b66dae7-140e-5116-ac81-188561d113a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-48571" :question/problem "The function $U(t)$ models the number of users of a new mobile phone app, $t$ hours after launching the app. What is the best interpretation of $U^{′}(4) = 650$?" :question/worked-solution "The function ${U}^{′}(t)$ represents the rate at which the number of users is changing at the moment $t$ hours after launch. \n\nTherefore, ${U}^{′}(4)=650$ means:\n\n$\\,$ *$4$ hours after the launch, the number of users increased at a rate of $650$ users per hour*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c2e340-6269-54f7-884b-bbecd7eff315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Degrees Fahrenheit per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242761} {:answer/id #uuid "d594dbda-4418-55d9-ba58-898386a7f9df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Degrees Fahrenheit" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242762} {:answer/id #uuid "49ff5265-6630-580c-8e27-ec4f27e9015f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Minutes per degrees Fahrenheit" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242763} {:answer/id #uuid "93cdb516-7609-54e6-b178-f441ba4f3179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242764} {:answer/id #uuid "8b85bcca-bb0b-57cf-b2dd-64c1e8c0faeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Degrees Fahrenheit squared per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29c2e340-6269-54f7-884b-bbecd7eff315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Degrees Fahrenheit per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242761} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dfa8ed1-b6b2-537b-a3de-0240dcb1c95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "453a4f9f-dc2c-5ef4-9ab6-f86f7acd693b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48611" :question/problem "A cup of coffee sitting on a teacher's desk $t$ minutes after it has been made has the temperature $F(t)$ degrees Fahrenheit. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have that the temperature $F(t)$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and the time is measured in minutes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t): & \\text{degrees Fahrenheit} \\\\ t: & \\text{minutes}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}$ measures a change in $F(t)$ over a change in $t.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{degrees Fahrenheit}}{\\text{minutes}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}$ is measured in \"degrees Fahrenheit per minute\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "159b4d58-b34d-52cd-aad2-2c058f772ad2" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9334e32a-7902-5dc5-8710-2edb1767639d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The area of a rectangle with base $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063012} {:answer/id #uuid "95f5013e-834e-5464-b086-5cd8bd550c24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the height changes as the area changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063013} {:answer/id #uuid "9f15d574-fb5e-5d2f-a11f-688ff88c353b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the area changes as the height changes\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"interpreting-the-derivative-of-a-function-in-a-given-context\"></a>" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063014} {:answer/id #uuid "a374ec78-feae-5ba2-b46f-b697e6f892dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the area changes as the base changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063015} {:answer/id #uuid "b29a1b41-8848-5080-aad0-e40ee42dd1b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The length of the base of the rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063016} {:answer/id #uuid "55d11992-046e-5370-b3a2-85e32fe92e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the area changes as the height changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a374ec78-feae-5ba2-b46f-b697e6f892dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the area changes as the base changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063015} :answer-field/id #uuid "08c21104-ac4e-5448-88ea-daebe5e7a776" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3408555-de68-5409-ab55-f212404a9d43" :question/math-academy-id "q-48684" :question/problem "Consider a rectangle such that the height is fixed but its base $b$ can vary. What is the best interpretation of $A^{′}(b)$ if $A$ is the area of the rectangle?" :question/worked-solution "The function $A(b)$ gives the area of a rectangle with base $b.$ So\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(b)=\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}b}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }A}{\\text{Change in }b}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${A}^{′}(b)$ is the rate at which the area changes as the base changes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "159b4d58-b34d-52cd-aad2-2c058f772ad2" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b90a7678-627b-5123-a419-b9f9ab424e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the volume changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242769} {:answer/id #uuid "284d5925-29e7-5bcb-9fcb-15203b779b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which each side changes as the volume changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242770} {:answer/id #uuid "7066fbd2-b7b1-5bd7-91e7-0b16c6ac3262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The volume of a cube with side $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242771} {:answer/id #uuid "99a520fe-dadc-522a-aa80-4d25590bf223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the surface area of the cube changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242772} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a2ba14-7966-5563-bf50-a87f3f58c287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The length of each side of the cube" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b90a7678-627b-5123-a419-b9f9ab424e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the volume changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242769} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f8920b-767d-5ef1-a1a7-07584549e425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8e8aeb3-9cec-5be3-983b-384590e62269" :question/math-academy-id "q-48685" :question/problem "Consider a cube such that the length of each side $s$ varies. What is the best interpretation of ${V}^{′}(s)$ if $V$ is the volume of the cube?" :question/worked-solution "The function $V(s)$ gives the volume of a cube with side $s.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }V}{\\text{Change in }s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${V}^{′}(s)$ is the rate at which the volume changes as each side changes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "159b4d58-b34d-52cd-aad2-2c058f772ad2" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "411ad8d5-f816-5f44-a0f5-502eae0d5ba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which each side changes as the area changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242776} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d4311e-60bd-5645-9cac-22c6bb0d2d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the area changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242777} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3e6b29-c0d1-5d7d-938d-211f20a2c517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which each side of the square changes as the perimeter changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242778} {:answer/id #uuid "c86889f3-39d8-527b-97ac-404d21630ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The length of each side of the square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242779} {:answer/id #uuid "be0a1e07-5aa6-5ad0-a87c-601be7ad5fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The area of a square with side $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "411ad8d5-f816-5f44-a0f5-502eae0d5ba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which each side changes as the area changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242776} :answer-field/id #uuid "6557a90c-c3fe-5f30-863f-1e01e83c3d1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83e86e86-7095-5890-8581-d695777d5884" :question/math-academy-id "q-48686" :question/problem "Consider a square such that its area $A$ can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${s}^{′}(A)$ if $s$ is the length of each side of the square?" :question/worked-solution "The function $s(A)$ gives the length of each side of a square with area $A.$ So,\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(A)=\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}A}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }s}{\\text{Change in }A}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${s}^{′}(A)$ is the rate at which each side changes as the area changes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2451a654-2255-5867-871d-54340dfeff81" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function in a Given Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d493ce6b-b21b-5ba7-b4fc-4ba82cf9431f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The distance traveled increased at a constant rate of $3$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242783} {:answer/id #uuid "4de00de0-f6a6-5f5b-ad2f-9b0b63a1f867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ seconds after the start of the journey, the distance traveled decreased at a rate of $10$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242784} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9c7f5a-594e-59e3-9a9b-ee8e7804baf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ seconds after the start of the journey, the car traveled $10$ meters." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242785} {:answer/id #uuid "71c660ad-dfa1-5767-a540-17834059be40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The distance traveled increased by $10$ meters during the first $30$ seconds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242786} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0776ab-c8e5-5659-b6dd-922c568208ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ seconds after the start of the journey, the distance traveled increased at a rate of $10$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a0776ab-c8e5-5659-b6dd-922c568208ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ seconds after the start of the journey, the distance traveled increased at a rate of $10$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242787} :answer-field/id #uuid "a56a0d24-326b-5d10-9e27-60d559cd68ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a182d83d-4491-54ee-b6e3-286916375314" :question/math-academy-id "q-48688" :question/problem "The function $x(t)$ models the number of meters traveled by a car, $t$ seconds after it sets off on a journey. What is the best interpretation of ${x}^{′}(30)=10?$" :question/worked-solution "The function ${x}^{′}(t)$ represents the rate at which the number of meters is changing at the moment $t$ seconds after starting its journey.\n\nTherefore, ${x}^{′}(30)=10$ means:\n\n$\\,$ *$30$ seconds after the start of the journey, the distance traveled increased at a rate of $10$ meters per second*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2451a654-2255-5867-871d-54340dfeff81" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function in a Given Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "784b9e8a-e0fd-5fe6-bf52-a2d4af4597b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "When $4$ liters of lemonade are sold the previous day, the price increases at a rate of $50$ cents per liter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242790} {:answer/id #uuid "75612cc7-fbb8-5e3a-98a7-d4ea244e6fe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "When $4$ liters of lemonade are sold the previous day, the price decreases at a rate of $50$ cents per liter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242791} {:answer/id #uuid "0573ab4c-9065-5fe3-8572-e749666f737e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "When the price of lemonade equals $50$ cents, the amount sold increases at a rate of $4$ liters per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242792} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0304ee-f353-5e3c-97e0-9d2e6b09f1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "When the price of lemonade equals $50$ cents, the amount sold decreases at a rate of $4$ liters per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242793} {:answer/id #uuid "cee63f7e-1ffe-5ce5-99e2-aa50831ad08a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "When $4$ liters of lemonade were sold the previous day, the price of lemonade is equal to $50$ cents." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "784b9e8a-e0fd-5fe6-bf52-a2d4af4597b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "When $4$ liters of lemonade are sold the previous day, the price increases at a rate of $50$ cents per liter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242790} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd376165-2458-583e-afbd-e9d29204103a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ab35a06-9856-5b30-a012-87953b9c2934" :question/math-academy-id "q-48691" :question/problem "The function $P(v)$ models the price of lemonade sold at a lemonade stand, in dollars, depending on the volume $v$ sold the previous day, in liters. What is the best interpretation of the statement below?\n\n$\\,$ *The slope of the tangent line to the graph of $P(v)$ at $v=4$ is equal to $0.5.$*" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the line tangent to the graph of $P(v)$ at $v=4$ gives the derivative of $P(v)$ at $v=4.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(4)=0.5.\n$$\n\nWe can interpret the above statement as\n\n$\\,$ *When $4$ liters of lemonade are sold the previous day, the price is increasing at a rate of $50$ cents per liter.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2451a654-2255-5867-871d-54340dfeff81" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function in a Given Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79e31126-c64b-55ab-8486-1ba7ff2737ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ hours after launching, the number of visitors increased by a total of $1\\,500$ visitors" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242797} {:answer/id #uuid "55e03b7f-4414-54d0-b71c-6553b529d974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ hours after launching, the number of visitors increased at a rate of $1\\,500$ visitors per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242798} {:answer/id #uuid "841b99eb-cf15-582c-97da-9e223d2c538a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ hours after launching, the number of visitors increased at a constant rate of $150$ visitors per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242799} {:answer/id #uuid "b68b01f1-43c1-56be-b56c-d0b60bca9c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ hours after launching, the number of visitors decreased by a total of $1\\,500$ visitors" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242800} {:answer/id #uuid "65abbacb-17ed-501e-9abb-0ad41fce9186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ hours after launching, the number of visitors decreased at a rate of $1\\,500$ visitors per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55e03b7f-4414-54d0-b71c-6553b529d974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ hours after launching, the number of visitors increased at a rate of $1\\,500$ visitors per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242798} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc44fb7a-ed51-5c60-bb1c-b037823cb5f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae6a6d2c-9c8c-5d28-9747-f12d076513b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48709" :question/problem "The function $V(t)$ models the number of visitors, in thousands, to a new website, $t$ hours after the website's launch. What is the best interpretation of the statement below?\n\n$\\,$ *The slope of the tangent line to the graph of $V(t)$ at $t=10$ is equal to $1.5.$*" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the line tangent to the graph of $V(t)$ at $t=10$ gives the derivative of $V(t)$ at $t=10.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(10)=1.5.\n$$\n\nWe can interpret the above statement as\n\n$\\,$ *$10$ hours after launching, the number of visitors increased at a rate of $1\\,500$ visitors per hour.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7df31c9-b27a-5ce2-93c8-178ba96da000" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca05b618-dfad-58d4-9bc2-1b78bb710962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$900$ bacteria per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242804} {:answer/id #uuid "1726b60e-7c2c-5476-aa9d-984c054238f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$100\\cdot {2}^{6}$ bacteria per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242805} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a9f4ef-9794-517f-8c21-aa32ce7b081e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$800$ bacteria per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242806} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1edb86-7e8a-50db-a8fa-ab13152e467a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$600$ bacteria per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242807} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a9a7e6-4158-5aaf-b9f5-c4947d0d168e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$700$ bacteria per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0a9f4ef-9794-517f-8c21-aa32ce7b081e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$800$ bacteria per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242806} :answer-field/id #uuid "c12405c7-ee47-521c-8a72-67068921111a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6272ab92-0dde-5f83-9c42-f6a326bcfe0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48902" :question/problem "The growth rate, $G(t)$, of the number of bacteria in a culture can be modeled by the function $G(t)=100\\cdot {2}^{0.5t}$, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since a sample of bacteria was placed in a Petri dish.  What will be the growth rate of the bacteria after $6$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "The function $G(t)$ models the growth rate for the bacteria $t$ hours since the sample was put in the Petri dish.\n\nThus, the growth rate of the bacteria culture after $6$ hours is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G(6) & =100\\cdot {2}^{0.5(6)} \\\\  & =100\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =100\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =800.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth rate of the bacteria is $800$ bacteria per hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7df31c9-b27a-5ce2-93c8-178ba96da000" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72f2967a-5ca3-5036-ba65-edb807d3f829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-94$ elephants per year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242811} {:answer/id #uuid "83708dce-bca1-51e5-a9b6-ac5e6d385956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-200$ elephants per year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242812} {:answer/id #uuid "b76ed14c-7b5a-5f9a-bcba-df8e22c676f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$240$ elephants per year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242813} {:answer/id #uuid "8476f099-5a13-502e-9148-09ec33c52a9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-220$ elephants per year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242814} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd12e8c-9149-52e2-8de7-d0b9163998fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$420$ elephants per year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83708dce-bca1-51e5-a9b6-ac5e6d385956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-200$ elephants per year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242812} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b8f2670-bb28-57ed-ad70-3f689e6286f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6be9d85b-4bff-5550-ab8a-64c0eeb04a36" :question/math-academy-id "q-48914" :question/problem "The birth rate of a herd of elephants in a region in Africa is modeled by the function  $B(t)=500\\cdot {2}^{0.125t}$ while the rate of death is modeled by $D(t)=400\\cdot {3}^{0.125t}.$ In both cases, $t$ represents the number of years since $1990.$  \n\nWhat was the rate of change of the elephant population in that region in $1998?$" :question/worked-solution "The  rate of change in the number of elephants  $t$ years since $1990$ is equal to the difference between the two given rates, that is\n\n$$\nB(t)-D(t).\n$$\n\nAs $1998$ is $8$ years after $1990$, we have $t=8.$  Hence, the  rate of change in the elephant population in that region in $1998$ was \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(8)-D(8) & =500\\cdot {2}^{0.125(8)}-400\\cdot {3}^{0.125(8)} \\\\  & =500\\cdot 2-400\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the elephant population was $-200$ elephants per year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9257a3f9-f902-5951-a0d3-dcc3648fd723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t) = r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063653} {:answer/id #uuid "adfb55f8-1dd2-5264-8cff-2cfb53da8dbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t) = r^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063654} {:answer/id #uuid "eaca6459-c7d5-512c-a227-55adcacfb17c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N^{′}(t) = r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063655} {:answer/id #uuid "096eb4d2-7405-581c-ac78-be059cbfd79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N^{′}(t) =-r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063656} {:answer/id #uuid "7301575d-bb4c-594d-9d18-dbc7d5952002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t) =-r^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaca6459-c7d5-512c-a227-55adcacfb17c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N^{′}(t) = r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063655} :answer-field/id #uuid "a869aa6e-9434-5eed-8b55-f676d6b8d396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97f8f938-0d50-59f5-922b-f7bea8bb44f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-72004" :question/problem "Jason, a biologist, is measuring the growth of bacteria in a Petri dish by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t$, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since he started his observations:\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of bacteria in the dish.\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the number of bacteria in the dish is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $N(t)$ and $r(t)$?" :question/worked-solution "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of bacteria inside the dish after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }N}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of bacteria is increasing at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $N(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${N}^{′}(t)=r(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eac346a-a06a-5869-88e7-54f432a6c94e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "euros" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242818} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ff81dd-4296-5cb2-bcfc-97e955b6b296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dollars per euro squared" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242819} {:answer/id #uuid "e545fdcb-87b0-5f1f-9c7c-372e91a7fa6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dollars per euro" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242820} {:answer/id #uuid "ead2afeb-061f-5c0a-951b-914b3b52d9a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "euros per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242821} {:answer/id #uuid "664eec34-c3d0-58a2-a38f-239a0945eed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dollars" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ead2afeb-061f-5c0a-951b-914b3b52d9a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "euros per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242821} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f2a632e-2c45-574c-90be-a380f5f91b61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7bd1f02-4969-5a75-a910-139f379407d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-72092" :question/problem "A bank exchanges $D$ dollars for $E(D)$ euros. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}D}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have that the currency $E(D)$ is measured in euros and the currency $D$ is measured in dollars. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(D): & \\text{euros} \\\\ D: & \\text{dollars}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}D}$ measures a change in $E(D)$ over a change in $D.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}D}: & \\frac{\\text{euros}}{\\text{dollars}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}D}$ is measured in \"euros per dollar\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "159b4d58-b34d-52cd-aad2-2c058f772ad2" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93be0bc2-fbb6-54d3-969a-0f2b07723d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the radius changes as the volume changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242825} {:answer/id #uuid "d77745c4-ef36-5a80-9b4b-fd8d3c554f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the volume changes as the radius changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242826} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6d0b96-336f-5547-97a1-b57ff18b6a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the surface area changes as the radius changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242827} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f50b01-806b-50fe-ba97-84e4e143d524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the volume changes as the surface area changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242828} {:answer/id #uuid "f01c4c53-b36d-5c55-bc5d-400848923046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the radius changes as the surface area changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93be0bc2-fbb6-54d3-969a-0f2b07723d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the radius changes as the volume changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242825} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e574c1-2c4b-595a-9385-86a71ea2e758" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fdf680e3-0d0f-525f-8e37-64c868afcaa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72099" :question/problem "Consider a sphere such that its volume $V$ can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${r}^{′}(V)$ if $r$ is the length of the radius of the sphere?" :question/worked-solution "The function $r(V)$ gives the length of the radius of a sphere with volume $V.$ So,\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(V)=\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}V}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }r}{\\text{Change in }V}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${r}^{′}(V)$ is the rate at which the radius changes as the volume changes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "159b4d58-b34d-52cd-aad2-2c058f772ad2" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a60668f8-0fa9-56d2-94b4-7aacdcb84ec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which each side changes as the area changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242832} {:answer/id #uuid "72e728c7-c801-5465-add3-a3cdae9193d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the perimeter changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242833} {:answer/id #uuid "5922755b-1461-5a2f-b42d-c95d924e22c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the area changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242834} {:answer/id #uuid "e6766686-6bb8-5c30-a317-cb6ed301ccfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the perimeter changes as the area changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242835} {:answer/id #uuid "107a582b-a0a1-5417-a6af-454af2c5f1eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which each side changes as the perimeter changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72e728c7-c801-5465-add3-a3cdae9193d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the perimeter changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242833} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d8cceba-4311-5d7b-b1ce-7b75f4b60c56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a33f008b-7cdb-5e9d-9988-28f511990924" :question/math-academy-id "q-72104" :question/problem "Consider a regular pentagon such that the length $s$ of each side varies. What is the best interpretation of ${P}^{′}(s)$ if $P$ is the perimeter of the pentagon?" :question/worked-solution "The function $P(s)$ gives the perimeter of a pentagon with side $s.$ So,\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }P}{\\text{Change in }s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${P}^{′}(s)$ is the rate at which the perimeter changes as each side changes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b46f0f24-6d9e-549f-8848-f1663eacb939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)=-{r}^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242839} {:answer/id #uuid "d12164b8-9d2c-5d1a-ab61-631749bf75f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242840} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf74d08-0212-5e39-86b0-ec2bd8ad9960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{′}(t)=-r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242841} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5d70de-bd2d-5bd4-a865-b9309f43cf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)={r}^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242842} {:answer/id #uuid "82809fdc-c24c-50c4-8813-dae75288d836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{′}(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82809fdc-c24c-50c4-8813-dae75288d836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{′}(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242843} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e91599e-df61-5bb8-93c0-39c95a687c88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "506715ad-75a6-5e80-881e-9c5f31214250" :question/math-academy-id "q-72105" :question/problem "Douglas, a pilot, is flying a plane. The plane's control systems record two measurements for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in minutes, since take off. The two measurements are:\n\n- $A(t)$ - the altitude (height) of the plane\n\n- $r(t)$ the rate at which the altitude is increasing\n\nWhat is the relationship between $A(t)$ and $r(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $A(t)$ gives the altitude of the plane after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}A(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }A}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${A}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the altitude is increasing at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $A(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${A}^{′}(t)=r(t).$"}]}
